{"causes": ["The intellectual climate of the Roman Imperial period fostered the compilation of encyclopedic works summarizing vast bodies of knowledge.", "The ongoing tradition of philosophical schools in Greece and Rome created a demand for accessible summaries of their founders' lives and teachings.", "The Hellenistic era's emphasis on biographical and doxographical writing provided a rich tradition and source material for later compilers like Laërtius."], "effects": ["Diogenes Laërtius's *Lives* became the primary conduit for the transmission of much ancient Greek philosophy to later generations, particularly during the Byzantine era and Renaissance.", "His work directly influenced the understanding and interpretation of numerous Hellenistic philosophical schools, including Stoicism, Epicureanism, and Cynicism.", "The biographical anecdotes and philosophical summaries preserved by Laërtius continue to be a foundational resource for modern scholarship on ancient Greek thought."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "diogenes-laërtius", "sourceName": "Diogenes Laërtius", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "lives-and-opinions-of-eminent-philosophers", "targetName": "Lives and Opinions of Eminent Philosophers", "context": "Diogenes Laërtius authored the ten-book biographical and doxographical work *Lives and Opinions of Eminent Philosophers* in the 3rd century CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "diocles-of-magnesia", "sourceName": "Diocles of Magnesia", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "diogenes-laërtius", "targetName": "Diogenes Laërtius", "context": "Diocles of Magnesia's earlier biographical and doxographical works served as a significant source for Diogenes Laërtius's *Lives*."}, {"sourceSlug": "diogenes-laërtius", "sourceName": "Diogenes Laërtius", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "epicurus", "targetName": "Epicurus", "context": "Diogenes Laërtius's *Lives* preserves nearly all surviving primary texts and biographical information regarding the philosopher Epicurus."}, {"sourceSlug": "diogenes-laërtius", "sourceName": "Diogenes Laërtius", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "roman-empire", "targetName": "Roman Empire", "context": "Diogenes Laërtius composed his *Lives and Opinions of Eminent Philosophers* during the height of the Roman Imperial period in the 3rd century CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "diogenes-laërtius", "sourceName": "Diogenes Laërtius", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "renaissance-humanism", "targetName": "Renaissance Humanism", "context": "The rediscovery and translation of Diogenes Laërtius's *Lives* in the 15th century significantly fueled the revival of classical Greek philosophy during the Renaissance."}], "places": [{"name": "Greece", "role": "Cultural Origin"}, {"name": "Rome, Roman Empire", "role": "Period of Activity"}, {"name": "Byzantium", "role": "Preservation Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Diogenes Laërtius", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "diogenes-laërtius"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q59138", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diogenes_Laertius"], "tags": ["Journalists & Chroniclers", "cause-and-effect", "cultural-diffusion", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Diogenes Laërtius's *Lives and Opinions of Eminent Philosophers* served as a critical conduit for preserving and transmitting ancient Greek philosophical thought, directly influencing later scholarship and the Renaissance revival of classical learning. Without his compilation, much of our knowledge of figures like Epicurus and the Cynics would be lost.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-11T14:23:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43035768539", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Diogenes Laërtius was a biographer of the Greek philosophers. Little is definitively known about his life, but his surviving work, Lives and Opinions of Eminent Philosophers, remains a primary source for the history of ancient Greek philosophy. His reputation is controversial among scholars because he often repeats information from his sources without critically evaluating it. In many cases, he focuses on insignificant details of his subjects' lives while ignoring important details of their philosophical teachings and he sometimes fails to distinguish between earlier and later teachings of specific philosophical schools.", "newValue": "Diogenes Laërtius, a Greek doxographer and biographer whose precise dates remain unknown but is generally placed in the first half of the 3rd century CE, compiled the monumental *Lives and Opinions of Eminent Philosophers*. This encyclopedic work, spanning ten books, serves as an indispensable, albeit sometimes uncritical, primary source for the history of ancient Greek philosophy, preserving countless fragments and summaries of doctrines that would otherwise be lost. His compilation offers a unique window into the intellectual landscape of the Hellenistic and Roman periods, capturing the diverse schools of thought from the Presocratics to the Epicureans.

His *Lives* systematically organizes philosophers into schools, often following a “succession” model, presenting biographical anecdotes, philosophical tenets, and lists of works for figures from Thales to Epicurus. While Laërtius himself offers little original philosophical insight, he meticulously drew upon a vast array of earlier sources, including works by Diocles of Magnesia, Antigonus of Carystus, and Favorinus. His method, though often criticized for its lack of critical evaluation and penchant for gossip, preserved a wealth of information, from the eccentricities of Diogenes the Cynic to the complex physics of Democritus and the ethical teachings of Plato.

The enduring legacy of Diogenes Laërtius lies in his role as a crucial conduit for the transmission of ancient philosophy. For centuries, particularly through the Byzantine era and into the Renaissance, his *Lives* was a primary means by which scholars accessed the teachings and lives of figures like Zeno of Citium, Pyrrho of Elis, and Aristotle, directly influencing the revival of classical thought. Despite scholarly debates over his reliability, his work remains foundational for reconstructing the intellectual heritage of antiquity and understanding the varied facets of Hellenistic philosophical discourse."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-11T14:23:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43035768539", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Rise of print media and public discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The intellectual climate of the Roman Imperial period fostered the compilation of encyclopedic works summarizing vast bodies of knowledge.", "The ongoing tradition of philosophical schools in Greece and Rome created a demand for accessible summaries of their founders' lives and teachings.", "The Hellenistic era's emphasis on biographical and doxographical writing provided a rich tradition and source material for later compilers like Laërtius."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-11T14:23:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43035768539", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped public opinion and historical record", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Diogenes Laërtius's *Lives* became the primary conduit for the transmission of much ancient Greek philosophy to later generations, particularly during the 